WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.983
SPEAKER 1: Credit card
numbers, birth dates,

00:00:01.983 --> 00:00:05.340
phone numbers, names,
addresses, passport numbers--

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.070
what do they all have in common?

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.590
Cloud DLP can protect them all.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:11.550
How does it do it?

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:12.840
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.240
So by now, you know
that de-identification

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.940
can be accomplished with a
few different techniques,

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.340
each with their own
benefits and features.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.370
But you might wonder, how
does DLP know what's what?

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.120
How does it know when
it's spotted a phone

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.460
number, for instance,
in the data processes,

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.200
and how sure is it?

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.140
When DLP inspects data,
the results for any matches

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:46.190
include a certainty score
that we call the likelihood.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.620
The likelihood can be
one of five scores that

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.970
range from very
unlikely to very likely.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.770
Likelihood is
important, because it

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.740
lets you fine tune the scope
of your de-identification,

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.050
and weed out potential
false positives

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:04.890
by setting a confidence
threshold based on likelihood.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.380
How is likelihood determined?

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.830
Well, for each of the
120 built-in infotypes,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.390
this score is
determined differently,

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.150
depending on the corresponding
infotype detector.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.220
That's the detection
mechanism that matches

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.040
on a certain set of criteria.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.970
This criteria could include
pattern matching or checksum.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.670
It can even consider
the surrounding context

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.500
that can be
identified in the data

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.620
around the potential match.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.270
Time for an example.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.130
Take a look at this string.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.510
What do you think
we're looking at here?

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.560
Well, it could be a job title,
followed by an arbitrary

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.640
identification number.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.600
On the other hand, it could also
be a person's name and a phone

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.580
number.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.340
When we run this through a DLP
inspection job, as expected,

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.090
we can get two infotype matches.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.250
Kitty Walker is identified
as the infotype person name,

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.400
with a Likelihood
score of likely.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.975
And the 10 digit number
got matched to phone number

00:02:01.975 --> 00:02:05.580
with a Likelihood
score of possible.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.880
Now, let's take a
look at the same data

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.940
with some positive
context mixed in.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.086
For us, it's a lot more
obvious what we're looking at.

00:02:14.086 --> 00:02:17.330
And similarly, Cloud DLP
considers the context

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.070
surrounding the data,
and the Likelihood score

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:25.340
jumped to very likely for both
person name and phone number.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.740
DLP's use of context
makes it especially

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:31.100
effective for conversational
text and transcripts.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.320
Once data is inspected
and classified

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.840
by infotype and
likelihood, DLP can then

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.400
apply de-identification.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.670
So let's talk about one
of the most basic forms

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.250
of de-identification--

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.210
redaction.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.070
What's redaction?

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.080
When working with text,
redaction completely

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.460
removes the sensitive values
from your data without a trace.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:52.310
Poof.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:55.250
It leaves no clue as
to what was removed.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.410
Let's see how the DLP
API handles redaction

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.410
by looking at a request
from a Node.js app.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.230
When we use a DLP API
to inspect content,

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.320
we construct a request
object that looks like this.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.840
In the request, we of
course specify the item

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.090
that we want to scan.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.130
We also provide an
inspection configuration

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.890
that configures the
scanning process.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.620
Here, we can limit the scope
by providing a likelihood

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:22.560
threshold or any
particular infotypes

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.910
that we might want
to limit our scan to.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.920
Next, there's a
de-identification

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.480
configuration.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.840
This is where we specify
that, with each match,

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.530
we want to de-identify
it with redaction.

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:37.340
The response we get
for this request will

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.130
have the transform data in
an overview that includes

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.440
a summary of the inspection
and transformation

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.060
for each infotype found.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:49.180
For text, redaction is one
of the most simplest forms

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:51.070
of de-identification,
because it leaves

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:53.500
no trace of what was removed.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.960
The same concept can
be applied to images

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:58.210
using image redaction.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:01.510
In this case, DLP uses
optical character recognition

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:03.540
to find potential
text in an image

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.970
and overlays an opaque bar over
any suspected sensitive values.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.180
Let's take a look at
another Node.js request,

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.430
this time for this image.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:15.490
The request object is a
bit different this time.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.800
We, once again, provide an
inspection configuration

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:21.768
where we can indicate
likelihood and infotypes.

00:04:21.768 --> 00:04:24.310
We have the option of passing
redaction configuration options

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:25.000
here.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.020
But by default, it'll just place
a black bar only on the text

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.160
that it matches.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.240
And finally, we provide the
image converted to a string,

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.850
along with the file type.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:40.450
The response here will be a
new image, this time with bars

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.340
covering the redacted data.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.150
Boom.

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.520
Our image has successfully
been de-identified,

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.310
thanks to image redaction.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.070
So whether you're working
with text or images,

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:52.570
redaction is the
way to go when you

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.090
want to totally and completely
remove those sensitive values

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.010
from your data.

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.720
Although data identification
redaction is powerful,

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:00.970
I did mention that it's one
of the most simplest forms

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:02.350
of de-identification.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:05.890
Next time, we'll take a look
at a more complex technique

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:07.510
and talk about
de-identification using

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.460
crypto-based tokenization.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:10.130
See you then.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

